# Copy this file to .env (same directory as the docker-compose*.yml files)
# and fill in a real value. Never commit the real .env file — it's already
# gitignored (see .gitignore's bare `.env` pattern), same as backend/.env
# and blockchain/.env.
#
# `docker compose` auto-loads a .env file sitting next to the compose
# file(s) it's run against — no --env-file flag needed. This is the ONE
# variable every docker-compose*.yml in this repo shares (they all run the
# same Postgres, whichever one you're using), which is exactly why it lives
# here at the repo root rather than duplicated into backend/.env.example.

# Real gap found via a full engineering audit: every docker-compose*.yml in
# this repo used to hardcode POSTGRES_PASSWORD=youthchain directly in the
# file, committed to version control — including docker-compose.tls.yml and
# docker-compose.scale.yml, both of which are this repo's own documented
# production stacks. Anyone with read access to this repository has always
# had the production database password, permanently (git history doesn't
# forget it even after a later rotation), independent of whether Postgres's
# own port is ever published to the host.
#
# docker-compose.yml (local/dev) still falls back to the old "youthchain"
# default if this is left unset, matching that file's own "zero setup"
# design goal — fine for a throwaway local database nobody else can reach.
# docker-compose.tls.yml and docker-compose.scale.yml (both real production
# stacks) now REFUSE TO START at all without a real value here — there is
# no way to accidentally run production on the old default anymore.
#
# Set a real, random value before running either production compose file:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
